# Summary
Introduced: the commit added the media proxy endpoint and the WhatsApp media download helper without constraining media_id to the expected provider ID format, URL-encoding it, or allowlisting Meta media download hosts before attaching the tenant access token.
The commit adds an authenticated media proxy for WhatsApp conversation media. The endpoint reads message.media_id and passes it to download_whatsapp_media with the tenant channel token_ref. download_whatsapp_media builds the Graph API request with string interpolation rather than treating media_id as an opaque path segment, so a message media_id containing path/query characters can alter the Graph API request. It then reads media_info['url'] and performs a second GET to that URL with Authorization: Bearer <tenant Meta token>, without validating that the URL is a legitimate WhatsApp/Meta media download host. Because ordinary tenant operations users can create outbound media messages with an arbitrary media_id, a malicious or compromised agent can persist a crafted media_id and invoke the new media endpoint to make the backend send the tenant's Meta access token to an attacker-controlled URL if the crafted Graph request yields such a url field. This exposes a high-value tenant channel secret and can enable unauthorized WhatsApp API operations for that tenant.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit introduced a media proxy endpoint/downloader path that connects stored message.media_id to a tenant channel token_ref.
- [x] Confirm tenant-ops message creation accepts and persists arbitrary media_id characters instead of enforcing a Meta media ID format.
- [x] Confirm get_whatsapp_media_info interpolates media_id into the Graph API URL without URL-encoding it as an opaque path segment.
- [x] Confirm download_whatsapp_media trusts media_info['url'] and attaches the tenant Meta bearer token to the second GET without scheme/host allowlisting.
- [x] Produce a targeted PoC showing a crafted media_id is accepted and the tenant token is sent to an attacker-controlled URL returned in media_info.
## Report
Validated the application-side bug at commit 8f048cf1e748674d78a20635248dfe77edc8cc08. This is a Python/non-compiled network logic flaw, not a memory-safety crash; I still checked for valgrind/gdb availability with `command -v valgrind || true; command -v gdb || true`, which returned no paths. Relevant code evidence: `app/api/v1/routes.py:1092-1154` adds the authenticated media endpoint and passes database `message['media_id']` plus channel `token_ref` directly into `download_whatsapp_media`. `app/api/v1/schemas.py:67-76` defines `MessageCreate.media_id: str | None = None` with no pattern/format restriction. `app/api/v1/routes.py:1193-1214` validates only that media messages have some media_id/media_url and then stores `payload.media_id.strip()`. `app/services/whatsapp.py:189-195` builds `https://graph.facebook.com/{version}/{media_id}` by interpolation and sends `Authorization: Bearer <tenant token>`. `app/services/whatsapp.py:209-223` then trusts `media_info['url']` and sends the same bearer token to that URL without scheme/host allowlisting. Dynamic PoC used the real schema, route validator, and `download_whatsapp_media`; it intercepted only the first Graph call to avoid external network and returned a local attacker URL. The real second `httpx.AsyncClient` GET reached the local HTTP server and leaked `Authorization: Bearer EAATENANT_SECRET_TOKEN`. The PoC also showed httpx normalization makes a crafted `../oauth/access_token?...` media_id change the effective Graph path to `https://graph.facebook.com/oauth/access_token?...`, confirming path/query manipulation rather than opaque ID encoding. I did not prove a live Meta endpoint can be coerced to return an arbitrary attacker URL, but the vulnerable application behavior and missing URL validation/token forwarding are confirmed.

